00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:39.460
Covered call ETFs have seen massive inflows for one very simple reason. They solve a frustrating problem

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:45.140
that almost every intermediate investor eventually runs into. You want reliable monthly or quarterly

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:50.220
income from your portfolio but you don't necessarily want to sell your underlying stocks and trigger

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:56.080
capital gains or lose the long-term compounding potential of quality companies. The mechanics are

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:02.060
actually quite straightforward once you understand the moving parts. The ETF holds a diversified basket of

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:10.480
stocks, sometimes an entire index such as the S&amp;P 500, the NASDAQ 100 or the TSX 60. On top of that equity

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:15.960
portfolio, the fund systematically sells out of the money call options against those holdings.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:21.100
The premium collected from those sold calls is then paid out to you as monthly or quarterly

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:27.080
distributions. In exchange for that income, you keep the underlying equity exposure but you typically

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:33.560
capture only 70 to 85 percent of the full upside in any strong rally. That trade off is the core of

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:39.400
the strategy. What you get in return is a high current yield, often running between 7 and 12 percent

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:45.600
annualized depending on the volatility environment and the specific ETF. You also benefit from lower

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:51.100
overall volatility because that option premium acts as a cushion on the downside. When the market

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:56.640
sells off, the premium you collected reduces the net loss compared with simply owning the index outright.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:06.040
Typical management fees in 2026 have come down nicely and now range from 0.35 percent to 0.79 percent.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:12.220
A far cry from the 1.5 percent plus fees we were seeing just a few years ago when the category was

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:17.860
still relatively new. The average monthly distribution yield across the entire covered call category

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:26.360
currently sits around 8.4 percent and the beta for most of these products lands between 0.65 and 0.85

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:32.380
versus their benchmark index. That lower beta is exactly why many professionals use them as a

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:38.520
volatility reduction tool inside an otherwise growth-oriented portfolio. On the Canadian side,

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:43.340
some of the most popular tickers you'll see in investor accounts include the BMO suite,

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:53.840
ZWC, ZWU and ZWE. For NASDAQ exposure, new vines, QQC and QQC. To have built a loyal following,

00:04:54.300 --> 00:05:02.140
Horizons also offers HXDM and HXD in the covered call space. More recently, several enhanced covered

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:08.500
call ETFs have moved away from strict monthly option cycles and are now using 30 to 45 day options.

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:14.220
This slight extension allows the managers to capture higher premiums when volatility spikes

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:20.000
and gives them more flexibility in strike selection. South of the border, names that are easily

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:25.680
accessible through Canadian platforms include JEPI, still the heavyweight in the large cap U.S. covered

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:34.020
call category and its NASDAQ counterpart JEPQ. Global X continues to see strong usage of both XYLD

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:41.200
and QYLD, while RILD and K&amp;G round out many investor sleeves. The sheer variety now available means

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:47.220
you can tailor the strategy to almost any sector or geographic exposure you want. Now that we understand

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:52.500
the mechanics, let's get practical and talk about when these ETFs shine, when they disappoint,

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:58.660
and the critical tax rules every Canadian investor absolutely must know before allocating a single dollar.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:04.840
Covered call ETFs tend to shine brightest in sideways or only mildly bullish markets.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:10.200
The exact kind of environment we live through for much of 2025 and have carried into the first

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:15.980
quarter of 2026. They perform especially well when implied volatility is elevated with the

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:21.760
VIX trading above 18 or its Canadian equivalent showing similar stress. In those higher volatility

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.900
regimes, the premiums are fatter, which translates directly into higher distributions for you.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:32.620
The strategy also makes a lot of sense when you need tax efficient monthly cash flow inside a TFSA

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:38.040
or RRSP. Inside those registered accounts, the distributions are completely tax sheltered,

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:42.380
which is a massive advantage for Canadian investors who want steady income without creating a

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:47.800
tax headache every year. That said, these products do lag meaningfully in strong bull markets.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:55.120
Just look back at 2023. Many covered call ETFs delivered total returns in the 9-14% range

00:06:55.120 --> 00:07:01.360
while the NASDAQ 100 gained well over 40%. You are deliberately giving up some of that upside in

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:06.360
exchange for the income and the lower volatility. It's a conscious trade off, not a flaw.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:12.300
In sharp downward crashes, the premium cushion helps blunt the loss but it certainly does not eliminate it.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:19.000
If the market drops 25%, you might still be down 15-20%, better than the index, but still painful.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:24.940
On the tax side, things get more complicated outside registered accounts. In non-registered accounts,

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:30.620
the option premium is generally taxed as ordinary income, which makes these ETFs far less attractive

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:36.280
unless you're in a very low tax bracket. You also have to be mindful of the superficial loss rules if

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:41.420
you simultaneously hold the underlying individual stocks in the same account. That can create

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:45.240
unexpected tax complications if you're not careful with your cost-based tracking.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:51.300
When it comes to portfolio construction, I suggest limiting covered call strategies to a maximum of

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:56.940
25% to 35% of your total equity allocation. That keeps the income and volatility reduction

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:02.480
benefits without turning your growth engine into an income only vehicle. I like blending US and

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:08.300
Canadian covered call ETFs to help manage currency risk. Pairing them with growth-oriented ETFs or

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:13.860
individual momentum names helps offset the upside you're voluntarily sacrificing. Rebalancing that

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:20.440
sleeve once per year inside your TFSA or RRSP keeps things tidy and prevents any single strategy

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:26.260
from dominating. We're also starting to see the next evolution, AI-powered covered call ETFs,

