00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:51.880
So, how do you actually pick the right covered call ETF without spending hours buried in research?

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:56.720
Here's the exact five-minute checklist I give every listener who asked me this question.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:04.340
First, identify the underlying index or sector. Are you looking at the S&amp;P 500 NASDAQ 100 TSX composite

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:09.960
or something more focused like Canadian banks or energy? Second, check the current distribution yield

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:15.200
and, more importantly, confirm how consistent that payout has been over at least the past 12 months.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:22.500
Third, examine the option writing style. Is it monthly, weekly, or one of the newer 30-45 day cycles?

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:28.000
Fourth, review the management fee and any noticeable tracking error versus the benchmark.

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:32.720
Fifth, look at the historical upside capture during strong bull months.

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:36.740
60-80% capture is a reasonable target for most investors.

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:40.840
Sixth, assess the currency exposure, hedged or un-hedged,

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:43.300
and whether that fits with the rest of your portfolio.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:47.040
You can run through this entire checklist in just a few minutes inside

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:49.560
wealth-simple, quest-raid, or interactive brokers.

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:55.480
Most of these platforms display the distribution history and fee data right on the ETF detail page,

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:57.700
so the information is literally at your fingertips.

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:02.900
The key is never to treat covered call ETFs as a replacement for broad index funds.

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:06.680
Instead, blend them thoughtfully with growth ETFs,

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:10.480
so you still participate meaningfully when markets really start to run.

